## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a streaming, per-tenant UI with time-based playback, memory controls, and precise cleanup when tenants switch. It exercises useSyncExternalStore, memoized selectors keyed by (tenantId, timeWindow), bounded ring buffers to cap memory, React.memo for EventRow components, playback controls (play/pause and seek), and tenant switch cleanup.

## Key Concepts
- useSyncExternalStore for external streams
- memoized selectors keyed by (tenantId, timeWindow)
- Bounded ring buffer to cap memory
- React.memo for EventRow components
- Playback controls: play/pause and seek
- Tenant switch cleanup

## Code Example
```javascript
// Stream wrapper using useSyncExternalStore
function useAuditStream(tenantId) {
  const storeRef = useRef(null);
  if (!storeRef.current) {
    storeRef.current = createAuditStore(tenantId);
  }
  return useSyncExternalStore(
    storeRef.current.subscribe,
    storeRef.current.getSnapshot,
    storeRef.current.getServerSnapshot
  );
}

// Memoized selector keyed by (tenantId, timeWindow)
const selectBoundedEvents = memoize((tenantId, timeWindow) => {
  return (events) => {
    const cutoff = Date.now() - timeWindow;
    return events
      .filter(e => e.tenantId === tenantId && e.timestamp > cutoff)
      .slice(-120); // Bounded to 120 events
  };
});

// React.memo EventRow
const EventRow = memo(({ event }) => (
  <div>{event.action} - {event.timestamp}</div>
));

// Panel wiring
function AuditPanel({ tenantId }) {
  const events = useAuditStream(tenantId);
  const boundedEvents = useMemo(
    () => selectBoundedEvents(tenantId, 120000),
    [tenantId]
  )(events);

  return (
    <div>
      {boundedEvents.map(e => <EventRow key={e.id} event={e} />)}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests candidates' familiarity with DOM manipulation, fetch, WebSocket, and Service Workers in a practical offline-first scenario.

## Key Concepts
- **Offline-first UX**: Using IndexedDB and Service Worker for data persistence and asset caching
- **Real-time updates**: WebSocket integration with in-order delivery (sequenceId) and deduplication (noteId)
- **Efficient rendering**: Debounced renders and diff-based DOM updates to minimize reflows
- **Offline queue**: Storing writes in IndexedDB when offline, then syncing with exponential backoff on reconnect

## Code Example
```javascript
function debounceRender(render, wait = 120) {
  let timeout;
  return function(...args) {
    clearTimeout(timeout);
    timeout = setTimeout(() => render.apply(this, args), wait);
  };
}

class NotesWidget {
  constructor() {
    this.notes = new Map();
    this.pendingQueue = [];
    this.sequenceId = 0;
    this.render = debounceRender(this.render.bind(this));
  }

  async init() {
    await this.loadNotes();
    this.connectWebSocket();
    this.registerServiceWorker();
    this.setupOfflineQueue();
  }

  async loadNotes() {
    const res = await fetch('/notes');
    const notes = await res.json();
    notes.forEach(note => this.notes.set(note.id, note));
    this.render();
  }

  connectWebSocket() {
    this.ws = new WebSocket('wss://api.example.com/notes');
    this.ws.onmessage = (event) => {
      const { data } = JSON.parse(event.data);
      if (data.sequenceId >= this.sequenceId) {
        this.sequenceId = data.sequenceId;
        if (!this.notes.has(data.noteId)) {
          this.notes.set(data.noteId, data);
          this.render();
        }
      }
    };
  }

  async addNote(content) {
    const note = { id: crypto.randomUUID(), content, timestamp: Date.now() };
    if (navigator.onLine) {
      await fetch('/notes', {
        method: 'POST',
        body: JSON.stringify(note),
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
      });
    } else {
      await this.queueNote(note);
    }
  }

  async queueNote(note) {
    const db = await indexedDB.open('notes-queue', 1);
    const tx = db.transaction('pending', 'readwrite');
    tx.objectStore('pending').add({ ...note, retryCount: 0 });
  }

  async syncQueue() {
    const db = await indexedDB.open('notes-queue', 1);
    const tx = db.transaction('pending', 'readwrite');
    const store = tx.objectStore('pending');
    const request = store.getAll();

    request.onsuccess = async () => {
      for (const note of request.result) {
        try {
          await fetch('/notes', {
            method: 'POST',
            body: JSON.stringify(note),
            headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
          });
          store.delete(note.id);
        } catch (e) {
          await this.exponentialBackoff(note, note.retryCount++);
        }
      }
    };
  }

  async exponentialBackoff(note, attempt) {
    const delay = Math.min(1000 * Math.pow(2, attempt), 30000);
    setTimeout(() => this.queueNote(note), delay);
  }

  render() {
    // Diff-based DOM update logic here
  }
}
```

## What are Closures?

Closures are a fundamental JavaScript concept where inner functions maintain access to variables from their outer (lexical) scope, even after the outer function completes execution.

## React Use Case

```javascript
function Counter({ initialCount }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(initialCount);

  // This callback creates a closure over 'count'
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    setCount(count + 1); // Accesses outer 'count' variable
  }, [count]);

  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Count: {count}</button>;
}
```

## Key Benefits

- Preserves access to outer scope variables
- Enables powerful patterns in React development
- Maintains state consistency across component re-renders
- Supports memoization and performance optimization

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the candidate's ability to implement proper tenant isolation in a React application, correctly utilize Context + useReducer to model a small Redux-like store, and implement basic form validation. It ensures the candidate can wire UI components with minimal boilerplate while maintaining clean architectural patterns.

## Key Concepts

- React hooks: useReducer, useContext, useMemo, and useCallback
- Context isolation per tenant with automatic state reset on tenant switch
- Reducer patterns for field operations (add, update, toggle required, move)
- Memoization for performance optimization
- Lightweight client-side validation

## Code Example

```javascript
const initialState = { fields: [] };

function formReducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'ADD_FIELD':
      return { ...state, fields: [...state.fields, action.payload] };
    case 'UPDATE_FIELD':
      return {
        ...state,
        fields: state.fields.map(f =>
          f.id === action.payload.id ? { ...f, ...action.payload } : f
        )
      };
    case 'TOGGLE_REQUIRED':
      return {
        ...state,
        fields: state.fields.map(f =>
          f.id === action.payload ? { ...f, required: !f.required } : f
        )
      };
    case 'MOVE_FIELD':
      const fields = [...state.fields];
      const [moved] = fields.splice(action.payload.fromIndex, 1);
      fields.splice(action.payload.toIndex, 0, moved);
      return { ...state, fields };
    default:
      return state;
  }
}

// TenantContext controls active tenant
const TenantContext = createContext(null);

// FormEditorContext holds the per-tenant form state
const FormEditorContext = createContext(null);

function FormEditorProvider({ children }) {
  const { tenantId } = useContext(TenantContext);
  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(formReducer, initialState);

  // Reset state when tenant changes
  useEffect(() => {
    dispatch({ type: 'RESET' });
  }, [tenantId]);

  const validate = useCallback((fields) => {
    return fields.map(field => {
      const errors = [];
      if (field.required && !field.label) errors.push('Label is required');
      if (field.type === 'number') {
        if (field.min !== undefined && field.max !== undefined && field.min > field.max) {
          errors.push('Min cannot exceed max');
        }
      }
      return { fieldId: field.id, errors, isValid: errors.length === 0 };
    });
  }, []);

  return (
    <FormEditorContext.Provider value={{ state, dispatch, validate }}>
      {children}
    </FormEditorContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates understanding of closures, in-memory caching, and request deduplication—concepts that form the foundation of efficient API client design. It represents a practical pattern that reduces redundant network traffic while exposing a clean, simple API.

## Key Concepts

- **Closures** to encapsulate private cache state inaccessible from outside
- **In-flight deduplication** to coalesce concurrent requests for the same endpoint, avoiding duplicate network calls
- **TTL-based cache invalidation** using timestamp tracking to determine freshness
- **Async/await** for clean, readable asynchronous API exposure
- **URL construction** and normalization for consistent cache keys

## Code Example

```javascript
function createApiClient(baseUrl, ttlMs) {
  const cache = new Map();
  const inFlight = new Map();

  return {
    async get(path) {
      const url = new URL(path, baseUrl).toString();
      const now = Date.now();
      const cached = cache.get(url);

      if (cached && now - cached.timestamp < ttlMs) {
        return cached.data;
      }

      if (inFlight.has(url)) {
        return inFlight.get(url);
      }

      const promise = fetch(url).then(res => res.json());
      inFlight.set(url, promise);

      const data = await promise;
      cache.set(url, { data, timestamp: now });
      inFlight.delete(url);

      return data;
    },

    clear() {
      cache.clear();
      inFlight.clear();
    }
  };
}

// Usage
const client = createApiClient('https://api.example.com', 60000);
const users = await client.get('/users?limit=10');
```
